tensive and um you know microscope  intensive and costly  all right thank you next question here  is for daniel  is there advice on ice contamination  during transfer to the tem for battery  materials after fib and how long do you  take out the sample from fib and bring  it to the tem  yeah so that's that is a difficult part  of the process um it's the one that we  had to spend a lot of time in our  process development on  but in general my advice  for  for transfer between fib and tem is to  keep it  keep it under liquid nitrogen if that's  okay keep it under liquid nitrogen for  as long as possible  and minimize any of the  the nitrogen gas exposures such as when  you load it physically into the tip of  the dem  make those as quick as possible and  these are steps that take a lot of  practice a lot of manual  fine motor skills  and then  the other part the other question was  how long between the two microscope  for us we'd like to do it the same day  if possible but  often what happens is we spend the whole  first day of the experiment doing the  cryofib and lift out and thinning and  then we'll store it in liquid nitrogen  in a i think it's a 35 liter  liquid nitrogen storage door  overnight  and  so between generation analysis it's  sometimes it's up to 24 hours  all right thank you daniel next question  is for john  how do you transfer the air sensitive  sample to the tem  yeah that's that's that's a good  question too that's um a very critical  step so  uh i can't recall dan if in your slides  you showed the glove box attachment but  um what we also have is the vct the  sample transfer  um  unit well we have a flange on the side  of the glove box so traditionally uh you  know if people are trying to transfer  air sensitive samples they will  put it in a you know basically in a  mason jar and then put that in a bag and  then you know run that down the hall  quickly open it up  um and pump down uh the sem with minimal  exposure um but we can put the vct  directly on the flange which goes into  the glove box so we can do things like  open  open coin cells  uh under argon and then transfer them  under vacuum and cold  so you know it can be under high vacuum  and then we can cool it down even more  to increase their vacuum  and then  put that onto the sem so that's one of  the really nice things about the the  leica system is  you know your sample can never ever see  ambient conditions which is which is  really nice  thank you john we have one last question  here and it is for daniel  for one cryo tem eels work how long does  it take usually double the dormer excuse  me double the normal stem eels and do  you have a cryo tem holder that also has  an air free function  uh so for cryo eels the main time  constraint is  drift correction  obviously with the tem holder the the 66  that we use  it drifts at i think it's about a  nanometer per second or a little bit  less  but we do have to use heavy drift  correction  um and that adds i think it's about  20  of the total uh acquisition time so if  you have a  uh 25 minute acquisition it'll add  another five to ten minutes onto it and  then um  we don't have an  inert cryo  trans uh tem holder we just have the  uh cryo the guitar 626 and i forget what  the other model is but we have a single  tilt and a double tilt and both are just  cryogenic holders but i know that some  other  institutions have the  vacuum and the cryo capability  so i will add on that though that you  know the the cryo holder does have the  frost shield which even though it's not  you know air free it does give you  you know a little bit more time  to transfer a sample  yeah absolutely and we've transferred  lithium calcium all of sodium various uh  reactive metals and  very minimal if not equivalent to a  vacuum transfer holder  great well thank you again dr daniel  long and dr john watt for your time  today and for your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor leica microsystems for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

